⟦P0⟧Return greetings for multiple people⟦P1⟧In the last changes you'll make to your module's code, you'll add support for⟦P2⟧getting greetings for multiple people in one request. In other words, you'll⟦P3⟧handle a multiple-value input, then pair values in that input with a⟦P4⟧multiple-value output. To do this, you'll need to pass a set of names to a⟦P5⟧function that can return a greeting for each of them.⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧Note:⟦P8⟧ This topic is part of a multi-part tutorial that begins⟦P9⟧with ⟦P10⟧Create a Go module⟦P11⟧.⟦P12⟧But there's a hitch. Changing the ⟦P13⟧ function's⟦P14⟧parameter from a single name to a set of names would change the function's⟦P15⟧signature. If you had already published the ⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧module and users had already written code calling ⟦P18⟧, that⟦P19⟧change would break their programs.⟦P20⟧In this situation, a better choice is to write a new function with a different⟦P21⟧name. The new function will take multiple parameters. That preserves the old⟦P22⟧function for backward compatibility.⟦P23⟧In ⟦P24⟧, change your code so it looks like the following.⟦P25⟧returns a map that associates each of the named people⟦P26⟧with a greeting⟦P27⟧A map to associate⟦P28⟧with⟦P29⟧Loop through the received slice of⟦P30⟧, calling⟦P31⟧the⟦P32⟧function to get a⟦P33⟧for each⟦P34⟧In the map, associate the retrieved⟦P35⟧with⟦P36⟧the⟦P37⟧returns one of a set of greeting messages. The returned⟦P38⟧message is selected at random.⟦P39⟧A slice of message⟦P40⟧Return one of the message⟦P41⟧selected at random.⟦P42⟧In this code, you:⟦P43⟧Add a ⟦P44⟧ function whose parameter is a slice of names⟦P45⟧rather than a single name. Also, you change one of its return types from⟦P46⟧a ⟦P47⟧ to a ⟦P48⟧ so you can return names⟦P49⟧mapped to greeting messages.⟦P50⟧Have the new ⟦P51⟧ function call the existing⟦P52⟧⟦P53⟧ function. This helps reduce duplication while also⟦P54⟧leaving both functions in place.⟦P55⟧Create a ⟦P56⟧ map to associate each of the⟦P57⟧received names (as a key) with a generated